title: 9. Why one record, and how a builder of another graph marks its part
pid: 0aa940c0-0ca2-42b4-966c-ddd3da23761a
body: |2-
  What the code records (what changed, the contracts in 'docs/') the
  plan links to and does not repeat.
  First use: Cleo built part 4 of Vic's plan and could not write Vic's
  graph, where the plan is; she wrote "For Vic to record in the plan"
  in her report, and the plan still said her part was next. Rejected:
  the builder forks the part, which splits the one record (a fork Vic
  made by accident surprised him); the plan's owner marks it at its
  next mission, which may be days away while the plan reads wrong. Top
  puts the lines in because it reads every report and already writes
  in every graph when it absorbs their reports; it copies, it does not judge
  (ours, unsure).
  Rests on: read (Nygard: one decision per record, kept with the work;
  Oxide: a committed RFD describes how the system now works). Ours:
  editing in place, departing from Nygard and Rust, who keep old
  records and supersede them, because here Git keeps the history and
  a second note on one thing is a loss.
